> am I right to declare m as ordered like below?
>
> fit <- sem(model, data = data$ready, cluster = "cluster_id", ordered = c("m"))

Yes

> is there a way to estimate the model?

lavaan's new multilevel functionality is very limited, and does not allow for categorical endogenous variables at this time.

> 2) If I do not declare m as ordered, how does lavaan then estimate m? Is it a simple linear regression or probit regression? How can I then obtain thresholds?

With single-level data, it will be probit regression by default, and lavaan will estimate thresholds by default.  The technical paper contains details about the model.  Although it uses CFA and latent growth curve examples, it applies to how ordinal variables are handled in general, including your path analysis.

https://www.statmodel.com/download/webnotes/CatMGLong.pdf

> 3) If I wanted counterfactual effects, how would I tell lavaan to estimate m as a logit?

See the "link=" argument on the ?lavOptions help page.  It is experimental still, and I think you also need to explicitly request "estimator = "ml" for it to apply the logit link.

