>  it is possible to use all data if I consider dependencies among data (for example, I have read a bit about cluster robust SE and about 2-level modelling in lavaan).

Cluster-robust SEs are available in the lavaan.survey package, but that would assume the measurement properties of your instrument remain constant over time (i.e., "longitudinal measurement invariance").  The 2-level modeling available in the development version of lavaan would also make that assumption about the within-level factor loadings, although it gets more complicated in the multilevel framework.  For example, there is also cross-level invariance, and even invariance across clusters (occasions) would make certain implications about cross-level invariance.  Here is a recent article summarizing the issues:

https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.01640/full

> Can you please recommend the best way to deal with these dependencies? In specific, it would be great if you could provide me an example including a schematic diagram (or lavaan code) of the overall measurement model.

I would recommend reading about longitudinal measurement invariance, which analyzes the data in wide (multivariate) format rather than long (multilevel) format.  Jason Newsom's book on longitudinal SEM is excellent.  Here is a good 2010 teaching article (with path diagrams).

http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1750-8606.2009.00110.x/full

Terrence D. Jorgensen
