Hi,

Chi square difference test is used to compare two nested models, if they are not then the result here is misleading.

I think you should see it like this. You impose extra restrictions on your model, for example: your restricted model says all the intercepts are 0 while the original / unrestricted model freely estimates them. This means: fewer parameters to be estimated and more DF in the 'new' model. This automatically implies that the model's chisquare will go up (higher chi square means a worse fit, but not necessarily unacceptable). By doing the test you did, you check if this difference in model fit (i.e. difference in chisquare) is acceptable. Is my more restricted and simpler model acceptable? If it is, the test will have a non-significant p-value. So it might be a bit counter intuitive: in this test you're 'hoping' (usually) for a large p-value.

So summarised:
If the result of the test is a small / significant p-value, then you'd keep the model that has fewer restrictions (i.e. the model with more freely estimated parameters)
If the result is the other way around, then you can pick the model on which you put some more restrictions (e.g. the model that says all intercepts are 0).

In your case, you would keep the model with fewer restrictions due to the small p-value. So the model that should probably be picked here is the results_all1.

Hope this helps. Perhaps read: http://zencaroline.blogspot.be/2007/05/chi-square-difference-test-for-nested.html

I didn't read it through entirely, but it gives an easy to understand example I think.

