Anna,
Just a word of caution:  The chi-square difference tests offers no possible outcome that would direct you to reject the less-restricted model.  If you accept the more-restricted model, you are also accepting the less-restricted model because the former is a special case of the latter.  So, you have to be careful when thinking of the test as a comparison between two models.

You can also think of the test as a test of the hypothesis that distinguishes the more-restrained model from the less-restrained model.  If Model 2 is Model 1 with two parameters constrained to equality, then the chi-square difference test is testing the hypothesis of equality between these two parameters.

However, you are testing that hypothesis conditional on the specification of the less-constrained model.  The chi-square difference test itself cannot evaluate this assumption.  So, it is important that you independently evaluate the less-restrained model and draw a conclusion about its viability before interpreting a chi-square difference test.  When I computed the p value for your less-restricted model, I obtained a value less than .001.

Nothing prevents you from computing a chi-square difference for two badly fitting models.  However, the p values can be inaccurate in such cases.

Keith
